<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Beiyang Army</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1895">1895</a>–<a href="/years/1928">1928</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Beiyang Army was the Western-style army created by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a> from 1895 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-shikai">Yuan Shikai</a>, based in the northern region of Zhili. It became the most modern force in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and the basis of Yuan's power, allowing him to negotiate the abdication of the last emperor in 1912. After his death in 1916 its commanders split into rival warlord cliques.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/beiyang-army.jpg" alt="Beiyang Army" width="320" height="304" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Beiyang_star.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a>'s traditional forces, the Manchu Banners and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/green-standard-army">Green Standard Army</a>, had declined so far that in the 1890s much of the army still carried bows, swords, spears and old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matchlock">matchlocks</a>. After the Nian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiping-rebellion">Taiping Rebellions</a>, provincial officials raised private armies of braves, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeng-guofan">Zeng Guofan</a>'s Xiang Army and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-hongzhang">Li Hongzhang</a>'s Huai Army. The Beiyang Army grew from the Dingwu Army, formed in 1894 and trained at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tianjin">Tianjin</a> by Hu Yufen with German officers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-shikai">Yuan Shikai</a> took it over in late 1895 as the New Field Army, and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-zhidong">Zhang Zhidong</a>'s Self-Strengthening Army it became the core of the New Army.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Yuan's division was the only unit of the Wuwei Corps not destroyed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boxer-rebellion">Boxer Rebellion</a>, and from 1901 he was put in charge of rebuilding the forces around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-policies-song">New Policies</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empress-dowager-cixi">Empress Dowager Cixi</a>'s court. His divisions grew until by 1911 they were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s strongest force. Prince Chun dismissed Yuan in January 1909 and placed the military under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a> princes, but when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuchang-uprising">Wuchang Uprising</a> began on 10 October 1911, Beiyang corps were sent against it. Yuan then used the army's strength to negotiate both the emperor's abdication and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-yat-sen">Sun Yat-sen</a>'s surrender of the presidency.</p><p>As provisional president from 10 March 1912, Yuan made the Beiyang Army the national army to curb the provincial military governors. After his death in 1916 it split between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duan-qirui">Duan Qirui</a>'s Anhui clique and Feng Guozhang's Zhili clique, and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-zuolin">Zhang Zuolin</a>'s Fengtian clique these factions fought for northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a>'s National Revolutionary Army, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-kai-shek">Chiang Kai-shek</a>, advanced north from 1926, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-zuolin">Zhang Zuolin</a>'s National Pacification Army was pushed back into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a>. The National Revolutionary Army entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> on 6 June 1928, ending the Beiyang Army, though many warlord forces were absorbed or kept their commands under nominal Nationalist control until 1949. Its officers came largely from the Baoding Military Academy, its recruits mostly from the peasantry.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Beiyang Army” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The revolutionaries lacked the strength to defeat the Qing’s <b>Beiyang Army</b>, so Sun agreed to stand aside in favour of its commander, Yuan Shikai, if he secured the dynasty’s abdication.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-china">Republic of China · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Yuan Shikai, commander of the <b>Beiyang Army</b>, the most modern Qing force, negotiated the abdication of the last emperor in 1912 and replaced Sun Yat-sen as president of the new Republic of China, keeping the capital in Beijing.</blockquote><a href="/state/beiyang-government">Beiyang government</a></li></ul></section>
